window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][148] = "<h1>Appendix H. Backlog</h1>\n<p>Topics that are real but belong beyond APM level, optional additions, and candidates for the Second Edition. Nothing here is developed. The row IDs (A1, B7, E7 and so on) are used by the groupings below.</p>\n<span id=\"app-h-deferred-topics-first-list-a1-and-on\"></span>\n<h2>Deferred topics, first list (A1 and on)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Why it is deferred</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A1</td>\n<td>Org design for product teams</td>\n<td>Decision-maker is a director or VP, not an APM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A2</td>\n<td>Product operations as a function</td>\n<td>Function-building, not individual PM work; the role is named in C01.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A3</td>\n<td>P&amp;L ownership and business-unit economics</td>\n<td>APM interviews test unit economics sanity, not ownership</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A4</td>\n<td>Executive communication at scale</td>\n<td>APM needs clear updates; org-wide narrative is Senior+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A5</td>\n<td>Hiring and coaching PMs</td>\n<td>Requires reports</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A6</td>\n<td>Portfolio strategy</td>\n<td>Requires multiple product lines</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"app-h-deferred-topics-second-list-b1-and-on\"></span>\n<h2>Deferred topics, second list (B1 and on)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>B1</td>\n<td>Principal-level thinking: patterns of great PMs, long game, reading list, transition into senior thinking</td>\n<td>Out of bar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B2</td>\n<td>Promotion mechanics, performance-review calibration, re-leveling at Big Tech</td>\n<td>APM needs level mapping only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B3</td>\n<td>Unwritten rules and organizational politics at Big Tech</td>\n<td>Mostly inferred from outside; Senior-leaning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B4</td>\n<td>Career paths above L4</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B5</td>\n<td>Seventy-twenty-ten portfolio of bets</td>\n<td>A6 sibling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B6</td>\n<td>OKR setting and workshops</td>\n<td>APM uses OKRs; facilitating them is Senior+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B7</td>\n<td>Building and running a PM team</td>\n<td>A5 sibling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B8</td>\n<td>Hiring and interviewing PMs</td>\n<td>A5 sibling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B9</td>\n<td>Influencing senior leaders <strong>beyond APM depth</strong></td>\n<td>APM-depth part moved to C32.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B10</td>\n<td>Compensation and equity strategy</td>\n<td>APM needs offer evaluation only; career track covers it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B11</td>\n<td>Funding-stage milestones (pre-seed to Series A)</td>\n<td>Founder content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B12</td>\n<td>Founder-PM mindset</td>\n<td>Founder content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B13</td>\n<td>Customer advisory boards</td>\n<td>Program-level, Senior-leaning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B14</td>\n<td>Open-source product management</td>\n<td>Niche</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B15</td>\n<td>Developer relations strategy</td>\n<td>Niche</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B16</td>\n<td>Hardware and hardware-software product management, <strong>beyond a primer</strong></td>\n<td>A short primer returns in C27; deep treatment stays here</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B17</td>\n<td>Infrastructure and internal-platform PM strategy, <strong>beyond a primer</strong></td>\n<td>Primer moved to C27.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B18</td>\n<td>Strategy theater at organizational level</td>\n<td>APM sees it; fixing it is Senior+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B19</td>\n<td>Vision setting for a product area</td>\n<td>APM contributes; owner is Senior+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B20</td>\n<td><strong>Team</strong> morale and managing a team's workload</td>\n<td>Requires reports. Individual burnout, mentoring and imposter feelings were moved into Volume 1 and went back to Volume 2 (E9) when C38 was dropped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B21</td>\n<td>Remote and distributed <strong>team</strong> craft</td>\n<td>Team-level. Individual remote working moved to C32.10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B22</td>\n<td>Career decisions: stay versus leave</td>\n<td>Personal career, not product skill; career track covers what applies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"app-h-suggested-volume-2-groupings\"></span>\n<h2>Suggested Volume 2 groupings</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Leading a product area (A1, A2, B7, B8, B19)</li>\n<li>Business ownership (A3, A6, B5, B10, B17)</li>\n<li>Strategy at organizational scale (B1, B6, B18)</li>\n<li>Influence and communication at executive level (A4, B9, B20)</li>\n<li>Career above APM (B2, B3, B4)</li>\n<li>Specialist domains (B14–B16)</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"app-h-optional-additions-not-in-volume-1\"></span>\n<h2>Optional additions (not in Volume 1)</h2>\n<p>These are real needs for some readers, but not necessary for the stated goal. Each notes the trigger that would justify adding it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Trigger to add</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>F1</td>\n<td>Coding and algorithm literacy for PMs</td>\n<td>Target roles that test coding in the PM loop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F2</td>\n<td>Python and data analysis for PMs</td>\n<td>Readers moving into data-heavy or technical PM roles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F3</td>\n<td>Brainteasers and logic puzzles</td>\n<td>Evidence that target loops still use them (<code>Inferred</code>: now rare)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F4</td>\n<td>Advanced causal inference (difference-in-differences, synthetic control, variance-reduction methods in depth)</td>\n<td>Data-product or experimentation-platform roles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F5</td>\n<td>Deep advertising mechanics (auctions, bidding, measurement)</td>\n<td>Ads-product targets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F6</td>\n<td>Deep domain tracks: gaming, creator platforms, healthcare, enterprise pricing</td>\n<td>Domain-specific hiring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F7</td>\n<td>Regional companions beyond India (Europe, Southeast Asia, Latin America, Africa, Middle East)</td>\n<td>Readers outside India</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F8</td>\n<td>Interview prep for adjacent roles (technical program manager, product marketing, data PM, AI/ML PM)</td>\n<td>Readers considering a lateral route</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F9</td>\n<td>A refresh edition for time-sensitive claims</td>\n<td>After 12 months, using the verification log</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F10</td>\n<td>Community mock-interview operating guide (running peer groups, feedback norms)</td>\n<td>Readers practicing in groups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F11</td>\n<td>Founder track: funding stages, founder-PM mindset (from B11, B12)</td>\n<td>Readers leaving for startups</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"app-h-candidates-noted-while-writing-e1-and-on\"></span>\n<h2>Candidates noted while writing (E1 and on)</h2>\n<p>Added by the author while writing C15 to C19: material that was cut back to an APM-level primer because it goes deeper than this volume needs. E7 to E9 are the career chapters (resume and applications; offers and negotiation; the first year as an APM) that are reserved for the Second Edition, with E10 and E11 (company lenses for further companies).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>ID</th>\n<th>Candidate</th>\n<th>Where it came up</th>\n<th>Why Volume 2</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>E1</td>\n<td>AI tools in PM work: workflows, evaluation of AI-generated analysis, privacy tooling</td>\n<td>C16.10</td>\n<td>Moves quickly; Volume 1 keeps principles only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E2</td>\n<td>Hardware product operations: supply planning, certification, recall management beyond a primer</td>\n<td>C17.3</td>\n<td>Specialist depth beyond the APM interview</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E3</td>\n<td>Experiment design for two-sided markets (interference, cluster and switchback designs)</td>\n<td>C14, C19</td>\n<td>Needs statistics beyond Volume 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E4</td>\n<td>Platform regulation by jurisdiction (competition, data and interoperability rules)</td>\n<td>C18, C19</td>\n<td>Facts differ by country and change quickly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E5</td>\n<td>Incident management and reliability practice for product leaders</td>\n<td>C17.5</td>\n<td>Senior-level ownership</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E6</td>\n<td>Marketplace operations: trust and safety, supply quality programs, dispute design</td>\n<td>C19</td>\n<td>Specialist depth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E7</td>\n<td>Resume, portfolio and application mechanics for product roles (formerly C35)</td>\n<td>Dropped from Volume 1, 2026-10</td>\n<td>Personalized, hiring-process facts change quickly and are hard to verify</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E8</td>\n<td>Offers and negotiation: level, compensation components, evaluating and negotiating an offer (formerly C36)</td>\n<td>Dropped from Volume 1, 2026-10</td>\n<td>Mostly time-sensitive and company-specific; unverifiable at book standard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E9</td>\n<td>Landing and thriving: a full first-year playbook for an associate PM beyond the first 90 days (formerly C38)</td>\n<td>Dropped from Volume 1, 2026-10; its concrete core is C37.8</td>\n<td>Soft, context-dependent material beyond interview preparation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E10</td>\n<td>Company lenses and briefs for Uber and Atlassian</td>\n<td>A review of company coverage (2026-10-13)</td>\n<td>Barely mentioned in Volume 1 (two mentions each); a Second Edition candidate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E11</td>\n<td>Company lenses and briefs for Indian product companies (for example Flipkart, Swiggy, Zomato)</td>\n<td>A review of company coverage (2026-10-13)</td>\n<td>None is mentioned in Volume 1; relevant to an India-based reader; a Second Edition candidate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n";
